{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 44, :comments ["<b>And despite even this, when they are</b> etc. One must understand how the phrase ״וְאַף גַּם זֹאת״ — which is an expression of beneficence — can be justified in connection with what precedes it concerning punishments and afflictions. Perhaps it refers back to the beginning of the passage where He mentioned their iniquities and their walking casually with HaShem — even so, ״I have not rejected them״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along the lines of what I explained in the preceding passage: that HaShem gives a reason for the insistence of every wise-hearted person as to why He would exile Israel from His land and not establish for them a fixed judgment upon the land itself, turning their path from its course. He further adds <b>even also</b> this reason, namely, through the very act of bringing them <b>into the land of their enemies</b> and the land being forsaken of them — <b>I have not rejected them</b> etc. <b>to destroy them</b>. This is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Midrash Tehillim 79): that HaShem cast His wrath upon wood and stones to deliver the people of Israel, as in ״מִזְמוֹר לְאָסָף בָּאוּ גוֹיִם״ etc. ״שָׂמוּ אֶת יְרוּשָׁלַיִם לְעִיִּים״ etc. — which he composed concerning that event. Also, His mercies will be stirred and the attribute of justice will be overcome when He sees the supreme people, children of kings, brought low at the hands of their enemies; and this very lowering of theirs will prevent the attribute of justice from destroying them. And that is what the verse says: <b>And despite even this</b> — namely, this is the reason for bringing them <b>into the land of their enemies</b>: that when they are in the land of their enemies <b>I will not reject them</b> etc. <b>to destroy them</b>; and for this reason I chose such a punishment." "<b>To break My covenant with them.</b> This covenant is that He would not exchange them for another nation, which HaShem established in His statement to those who went out of Egypt. And that is what He concludes by saying <b>for I am HaShem their Elohim</b> — meaning, this is the sign of the covenant: their Elohim, and not the Elohim of another nation. Also His statement at the time of the giving of the Torah, that they shall be a treasured people above all peoples and a kingdom of priests — and the statement of HaShem stands in place of a covenant." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the verse's intention is along these lines: <b>I have not rejected them</b> etc. <b>to destroy them</b>, and the reason is for the sake of My covenant with them. He then returned and explained what the covenant is — <b>for I am HaShem their Elohim</b>: this is the covenant that would be broken if I destroyed them, for there is no fulfillment of HaShem's covenant that He be their Elohim — which they undertook to keep. It further intends the Torah, which is called a covenant: for if there is no Israel, the Torah that HaShem gave them, which is the covenant that is with them, would be broken."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:47.873676896Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}